NAME
       systemctl - control systemd: start, stop, enable and inspect services and targets

SYNOPSIS
       systemctl [OPTIONS] COMMAND [UNIT...]

DESCRIPTION
       systemd is the first program the kernel starts (PID 1). It starts and
       watches everything else, described in units: services (sshd.service),
       targets that group units (multi-user.target), timers, mounts, sockets
       and others. systemctl is how you talk to it.

       Two separate questions matter for every service:

       Is it running now?        start, stop, restart change that;
                                 is-active answers it.
       Does it start at boot?    enable, disable change that;
                                 is-enabled answers it.

       Enabling does not start a service, and starting does not enable it.
       Use enable --now to do both, which is what most tasks want.

       The .service suffix may be left out: systemctl status sshd works.

       Unit files come from packages in /usr/lib/systemd/system. Your own
       units and changes go in /etc/systemd/system, which wins over the
       package directory. After adding or editing a unit file, run
       systemctl daemon-reload so systemd reads it again.

COMMANDS
   Services
       start, stop, restart UNIT...
              Start, stop, or stop and start again.

       reload UNIT...
              Ask the service to read its configuration again without
              stopping (only services that support it).

       try-restart UNIT..., reload-or-restart UNIT...
              Restart only if running; reload if possible, else restart.

       status [UNIT...]
              Show whether the unit is loaded, enabled and active, its main
              PID, and its latest log lines.

       enable [--now] UNIT..., disable [--now] UNIT...
              Start, or stop, the unit at boot (by creating or removing links
              in /etc/systemd/system/*.wants). --now also starts or stops it.

       mask UNIT..., unmask UNIT...
              Make a unit impossible to start, even by hand or as a
              dependency (it is linked to /dev/null), or undo that.

       is-active, is-enabled, is-failed UNIT...
              Print the state and set the exit status (0 = yes). Good for
              scripts and tests.

       kill UNIT
              Send a signal to the unit's processes.

       reset-failed
              Forget units in the failed state.

   Looking around
       list-units [--type=TYPE] [--state=STATE] [--all]
              Units systemd has in memory (by default the active ones).

       list-unit-files [--type=TYPE]
              Every installed unit file and whether it is enabled.

       list-timers [--all]
              Timers with their next and last run.

       list-dependencies [UNIT]
              The units a target or unit pulls in.

       cat UNIT
              Print the unit file and its drop-in files.

       show UNIT [-p PROPERTY]
              Print the unit's properties as NAME=VALUE.

       edit UNIT [--full]
              Create a drop-in file (override.conf) for the unit and open it in
              an editor; --full edits a copy of the whole unit file.

       daemon-reload
              Read all unit files again.

   Targets and the system
       get-default, set-default TARGET
              Show, or set, the target the system boots into.

       isolate TARGET
              Switch to TARGET now, stopping what it does not need.

       rescue, emergency
              Switch to the rescue or emergency target.

       reboot, poweroff, halt
              Restart or switch off the machine.

OPTIONS
       -t, --type=TYPE
              Only units of this type: service, target, timer, socket, mount.

       -a, --all
              Include inactive units.

       --state=STATE
              Only units in this state, such as failed, running or enabled.

       --now  With enable or disable: also start or stop.

       --user Talk to the user's own systemd (rootless podman services, user
              timers) instead of the system one.

       -p, --property=NAME, --value
              With show: only these properties; --value prints just the value.

       -q, --quiet
              Print nothing; only set the exit status.

       --no-pager, -l, --full
              Do not page the output; do not cut long lines.

       -f, --force
              With enable or set-default: replace existing links.

EXAMPLES
       Install and start a web server now and at every boot:

           systemctl enable --now httpd
           systemctl is-active httpd; systemctl is-enabled httpd

       Find services that failed:

           systemctl --failed

       Boot into the text console from now on:

           systemctl set-default multi-user.target

       Make sure a service can never start:

           systemctl mask --now cups

       Reload units after writing /etc/systemd/system/backup.service:

           systemctl daemon-reload
           systemctl start backup

FILES
       /etc/systemd/system/
              Your units, overrides (UNIT.d/*.conf) and enable links.

       /usr/lib/systemd/system/
              Units installed by packages.

       ~/.config/systemd/user/
              A user's own units (systemctl --user).

SEE ALSO
       systemd.unit(5), systemd.service(5), systemd.timer(5), journalctl(1),
       systemd-analyze(1), loginctl(1)
